{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790323023,"nonce":"0FNZtt+rehwvS7rhXwmZapaq4tI=","title":"Lua GC: a stale word keeps a weak-key table's dead keys under ZYLUA_LLVM=1 with the collector stressed","message":"gc/weak_traversal.lua, the \"k next\" check, prints \"few left false\" in debug builds on the NUC (x86_64) under ZYLUA_LLVM=1 ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1: the collectgarbage() in the middle of the traversal leaves at least 50 dead keys in the table. Rates, interleaved on the same machine: the package before round 3 (2b683a63 on main 0f0e5585, debug, llvm-backend) 1 of 60; the round-3 tip (0044fd01) 7 of 60 and 3 of 30. Release builds: 0 of 60 for both, 1 of 40 once. Mac debug llvm-backend: 0 of 60. The three harness modes (plain, warm-up on, stressed, none of which sets ZYLUA_LLVM) pass 40 of 40 each on the NUC debug build.\n\nZYNTAX_GC_EXPLAIN=1 with the host also reporting weak keys a stack word reaches shows the dead keys reached as key \u003c- key box+8 \u003c- a block of dict entry pairs (+936, +952, +968, +984) \u003c- one stack word about 200 KB above the collection's stack pointer. The block is not the table's current entry storage (that one is held, never traced), so it is an older buffer of the table's dict. On x86_64 the frame-pointer walk breaks at LLVM-compiled frames, so the report attributes the word to call_native_through's frame, which cannot be trusted; the word may be in a compiled frame or in a live interpreter frame above them.\n\nThe round-3 interpreter clearing does not cover compiled frames. Next step: find the frame on aarch64 if it reproduces there, or record frame ranges of compiled code on x86, then either keep the old buffer from being named (free it when the dict grows or compacts) or clear the frame that holds the word.","files":null}]}